  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Tàihé A Bāo 泰和A包. Logic: First out wins; declarer wins or loses stakes per opponent based on bidding.',
    Player: 'Tàihé A Bāo (泰和A包) is a Chinese climbing game from Taihe county, Jiangxi province. Four players: one declarer vs the other three each hand. 40-card deck (remove 2s, 3s, 8s); rank A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 7, 6, 5, 4. Cards drawn from deck counterclockwise (not dealt). Bidding: declare or pass; if all pass, second round—call for a card (exchange); last caller is declarer. Play: declarer leads combinations; others beat or pass. Combinations: singles, pairs, sequences, double sequences, three-of-a-kind, four-of-a-kind (bombs beat any non-bomb). First out wins; if declarer wins they collect stakes from each opponent. Often played for money.',
    objective: 'As declarer, be the first to empty your hand to win and collect stakes from each opponent; as an opponent, empty your hand first to make the declarer lose and pay each of you.',
    gameplay: '1. Deal: Each player takes 10 cards one at a time in counterclockwise order from the face-down deck. 2. Bidding round 1: In turn, each player may declare (become declarer; play starts immediately) or pass. 3. If all pass, bidding round 2: Each may call for a card (name rank and suit; holder gives it face-up, you give one face-down in exchange) or pass; last player to call is declarer. If all pass again, redeal. 4. Play: Declarer leads any legal combination. Each in turn may beat it (same type, higher rank) or pass. Three consecutive passes clear the table; the last player to have played leads any legal combination. 5. First player out of cards wins the hand: if declarer, they win; if an opponent, declarer loses. Stakes are paid per opponent based on how many called in bidding.',
    keyRules: [
      'Combinations: single, pair, sequence (3+ consecutive ranks; Aces not in sequences; 7 and 9 not consecutive), double sequence, three-of-a-kind, four-of-a-kind. Three- and four-of-a-kind (bombs) beat any non-bomb.',
      'Rank order high to low: A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 7, 6, 5, 4. Suits do not affect rank.',
      'Hand value in stakes: if declarer called for a card, value = number of players who called; if declarer declared in round 1, value = number of players plus one. Declarer wins or loses that many stakes to each opponent.',
    ],
